=====================================================================

The PFM+ and PFM512 are replacements for the Geneve 9640 Boot EPROM.
The upgrade consists of one or two EEPROMS that allow the user to store both
the Operating System and a number of files in the flash memory. If installed
you will see a blue, 80 column screen in place of the Swan image.

    PFM+   - Initial release.  2 x 128K Atmel 29c010 EEPROMS
    PFM512 - Second release.  1 x 512K Atmel 29c040 EEPROM
                              Atmel 29c040a and Winbond 040 compatible

The files in this release are provided for you, the user, to upgrade the
PFM operating CORE (boot bios), install the OS, and install a flashdisk.

                ************************************
                            W A R N I N G
                ************************************
                          PREPARATORY STEPS
                ************************************

1. NEVER program the OS or Flashdisk via the PFM boot menu. These options will
       corrupt the system and the flashdisk.  Use the PFM utilities or CYA.

2. The program has been tested in multiple systems. If you have questions or
   concerns, please ask before you start the process.

3. Create a copy of the files on your existing Flashdisk. The OS and Flashdisk
   utilities will overwrite the current contents.

4. Create at least one emergency copy of the Geneve OS on a floppy. Verify
   the file's integrity before committing to an update.

5. Copy your AUTOEXEC to the Geneve OS floppy. You may need to execute this
       file manually from the command line using the ampersand "&"

6. If possible, test load the Geneve OS via the PFM boot menu. The boot menu
   can be accessed by holding the space bar at powerup. DO NOT program the
   OS via the menu!

7. Write down your device assignments, remaps, paths, and SCSMAP configuration.
   When you load the new OS, the settings will default to original state. The
   most recent CYA can be used to set the configuration directly.

6. Reminder: the AUTOEXEC will default load from DSK1. unless/until you
   modify the OS via CYA.

       File              Description
       ============      ================================================
       PFMCOREUP         BIOS Core updater/programmer. Use with CORE file
       COREV802          BIOS Core Revision 8.02
       ------------      -------------------------------
       PFMSYS            System/Sys (OS) loader for PFM device
       PFMPLUSF          PFM+ flashdisk loader
       PFM512F           PFM512 flashdisk loader
       PFMPROD           PFM device identifier; standalone

*New   PFMDUMPOS         Dump the installed OS (136k) from PFM to file
*New   PFMCRC            Check the integrity of the installed OS
       RAM111            Batch file to create ramdisk for PFM+ flash setup

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
The core/boot BIOS must be upgraded to support MDOS 7.40+

Be sure all necessary preparation is complete before starting this process.

------------------------
1. UPGRADE THE PFM CORE
------------------------

From the command line: PFMCOREUP <corefile>

             Examples: PFMCOREUP COREV802    (current path)
                       PFMCOREUP H:COREV802  (with path)

If the PFM device is detected, the program will load the core file and validate
its integrity. If successful, you will be asked to confirm the program step.
If you select "Y" to program, the new core will be loaded into your PFM.


------------------------
2. INSTALL THE OS
------------------------
If you are ready to install MDOS, proceed with this step. Although not
recommended, you may continue to use the old MDOS loaded in your device.

From the command line:   PFMSYS  <systemfile>

             Examples:   PFMSYS  SYSTEM"/"SYS    [quotes required for slash]
                         PFMSYS  H:HDOS

PFMSYS will load the OS into memory. The program will check the integrity of
the OS file. If the program reports an error or changes, consider obtaining
another copy of the OS.  (See also CRCOS7)

You will be prompted to program the PFM with the OS. If you select "Y",
the OS will be loaded within a few second.

You may run PFMCRC to validate the programming.

When finished, you may restart your Geneve.  Autoexec will load from DSK1
or default for the system file.

---------------------------------
3. Set Up The PFM FLASHDISK
---------------------------------
The flashdisk mimics a read-only ramdisk. Its capacity is based on the chipset

Capacity:   PFM+      444 sectors
            PFM512   1440 sectors

Use REMAP xT where x=DSK1-9, to access the flash device

The flashdisk is loaded by means of a sector-based device. Future
enhancements may allow for disk image files.


CHOOSE THE APPROPRIATE FOR YOUR SYSTEM:

PFM+ Flashdisk (Atmel 29c010 x 2)
---------------------------------
1. Execute the batch file with an ampersand "&" to create a 444 sector ramdisk
       Example:  "&RAM111"  will create the internal ramdisk
2. Remap a disk device  to the internal ramdisk e.g. REMAP 5E to remap DSK5.
3. Label the ramdisk volume, if desired.
4. Copy your files to the internal ramdisk
4. Run PFMPLUSF to copy the files from ramdisk to PFM+ flashdisk
            Example:     PFMPLUSF DSK5.
                         PFMPLUSF E:  (if E is assigned to DSK5.)
5. Confirm programming. When complete, the flashdisk is ready.
6. Remap a disk device e.g., REMAP 6T to assign DSK6 to the flashdisk

PFM512 Flashdisk
-----------------
1. Prepare/format a disk or initialize an internal ramdisk
     a) DS/DD disk, capacity 1440 sectors OR
     b) Horizon Ramdisk using GENCFG, select option for "1440" Disk.
            (Warning:  this will erase all data on the ramdisk)
     c) Internal Ramdisk size 360k, if you have expanded memory

2. Label the disk if desired
3. Copy file to the formatted device
4. Run PFM512F to copy the files to the PFM512 flashdisk
            Example:     PFM512F DSK5.  (copy from DSK5)
                         PFM512F A:     (copy from floppy)

5. Confirm programming. When complete, the flashdisk is ready.
6. Remap a disk device e.g., REMAP 6T to assign DSK6 to the flashdisk

